description:
To avoid a pull request, here are my tidy methods for xgboost & xgboost cv
## xgboost:

```
tidy.xgb.Booster <- function(x, ...){
  data.frame(x$evaluation_log[x$best_iteration])
}
```
## xgb.cv

```
tidy.xgb.cv.synchronous <- function(x, ...){
  data.frame(x$evaluation_log)
}
```

from the git documentation:

> We avoid including arguments that were given to the modeling function. For example, a glm glance output does not need to contain a field for family, since that is decided by the user calling glm rather than the modeling function itself.

It would actually be really convenient if model objects included the model specification. e.g. when you are comparing which model type/family/link works best.

I often want to compare many models-- usually collected in a list-- to each other.  I've currently implemented this in my `PKNCA` package with the non-exported `AIC.list()` function, but as I think of it more, `broom` (and `broom.mixed`) seem like a better home for the type of functionality I'm thinking of.

While I can accomplish the same thing with a call like the following (typed directly into GitHub; it may have bugs, but it hopefully indicates the intent):

```r
model_list %>%
  lapply(FUN=glance) %>%
  bind_rows()
```

For me, the multiple-model comparison need is common and I'd like to have an interface like:

```r
glance_list <- function(x, ...) {
  ret_list <- lapply(X=x, FUN=glance, ...)
  bind_rows(ret_list)
}
```

To make this work with models that don't converge, I'd also ideally like to have `glance()` methods for logical NA and NULL:

```r
glance.logical <- function(x, ...) {
  if (length(x) == 1 && is.na(x)) {
    # Choose appropriate "model summaries" for NA, possibly an empty data.frame, perhaps:
    data.frame(convergence=FALSE)
  } else {
    stop("Cannot glance at a logical value other than scalar NA.")
  }
}
```

```r
glance.NULL <- function(x, ...) {
  # Choose appropriate "model summaries" for NULL, matching NA
  glance.logical(NA, ...)
}
```

This relates to: billdenney/PKNCA#71 and billdenney/PKNCA#90

As described in #561, would you find it reasonable to add `glance()` (and maybe `tidy()` and `augment()`) methods for logical `NA` values?

```r
glance.logical <- function(x, ...) {
  if (length(x) == 1 && is.na(x)) {
    # Choose appropriate "model summaries" for NA, possibly an empty data.frame, perhaps:
    data.frame(convergence=FALSE)
  } else {
    stop("Cannot glance at a logical value other than scalar NA.")
  }
}
```

The rationale for inclusion is when generating a list of models sometimes logical `NA` is more convenient than `NULL`.  Also, different than `NULL` (where `glance()` returns an empty tibble, which is logical for `NULL`), it would help to have a row from `glance()` so that there is a 1:1 match between list elements and glance rows when `bind_rows()` or similar is run on the result.

As an alternate, the returned data.frame could have the attribute `NA_model` or similar set to `TRUE` to be even clearer about its source.

I think that `tidy.logical()` and `augment.logical()` should confirm a scalar NA value and then return an empty tibble.

